  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nIsolated showers continue to be possible through Saturday\nafternoon/evening. The 12Z sounding describes an environment\nwith saturated low levels up to 750mb, with a capping inversion\nseparating dry air above. This generally alludes to low\npotential for storms, with most of the saturated layer above\nfreezing and capping as a limiter for vertical development.\nForcing along a weak shortwave will exit to the east tonight,\ncutting off rain potential. The highest chances of rain are east\nof I-79, and low at that.\n\nThe subsidence cap is expected to erode some into the day \ntomorrow, which may make thunderstorm chances comparatively \nhigher with some chance at vertical development. In general, \nCAMS are more focused on development off of Lake Erie into \ntomorrow on a weak mid-level wave. The main question is will \nthese storms be capable of becoming vertically developed. Flow \nover western PA and northern West Virginia will be weak (~25kt \nsfc-6km shear at KPIT), while eastern Ohio may be a bit stronger\n(~40kt sfc-6km shear at KZZV) with most shear in the 3km to 6km\nlayer. Dry air above 700mb, may act as a double edged sword for\nany severe chances. For now, the most likely scenario is that \ndry air limits vertical development, does not allow cells to tap\ninto shear aloft, and minimized DCAPE actualization. In the \nless likely, but notable scenario where storms are able to push \npast the dry air aloft, they may be able to actualize ~600-900 \nDCAPE, making for downburst chances. The narrow window for this \nconditional threat favors 4p-8p. ML convective outlooks support \nthis conditional, low-end threat.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe highest severe thunderstorm chances persist for Monday and\nTuesday, as an Midwest ridge axis pushes closer and shunts a\nNortheast trough east. This will increase the chances of\nupstream development of an MCS that may cross the area. The\nfirst of such chances will come Sunday night into Monday\nmorning, but guidance continues to keep this first chance well\nwest of the forecast area into Michigan/Indiana/west Ohio.\n\nIt's worth noting that there are still a number of potential\noutcomes for severe weather into Monday and Tuesday. We will\nwalk through the two most likely scenarios:\n\n1) A MCS develops upstream during the day on Monday and passes\nnear the area sometime Monday / Tuesday morning. A later \narrival time lowers the chances of severe, but nocturnal severe \nremains possible. A direct hit would limit additional severe \nchances Tuesday with a cold front passage, while a glancing blow\nwould allow severe chances again Tuesday.\n\n2) A MCS develops upstream Monday, but misses the area to the\nwest. This would heighten the chances of severe on a cold front\nand/or tied to an MCS passage into the day Tuesday. \n\nAny severe chances would primarily bring the threat of damaging\nwind, though all hazards are possible including hail and \nisolated tornadoes. Though storms would likely be moving fast, \ntraining convection would heighten the flash flood threat for \nany locations that get hit multiple times. Severe threats will \nneed to continue to be monitored for more timing, intensity, and\nhazard updates. As often is the case with MCSs, this more \ncertain details may not be available until 12-24 hours before \nthe event, so stay tuned to the forecast.",
